{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Pegis1961-apbpcdp",
  "Source": "R. J. Pegis, \"An Exact Design Method for Multilayer Dielectric Films,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 51, 1255-1264 (1961). doi:10.1364/JOSA.51.001255 - Table V p. 1264 (refractive indices, row a'b'cd'); Table IV p. 1264 (surface reflectances, same row); Sec. IV pp. 1260-1263.",
  "Notes": "Exact (Fourier-series / continued-fraction) synthesis of a 5-layer equal-optical-thickness dielectric stack whose reflectance matches eq. (34): a ~3:1 high-reflectance band (g = 15-50 deg) with low reflectance outside; solution class a'b'cd'.\nSpecification: Ideal curve Fig. 2: |R|^2 = 100 % (1/T capped at 10, i.e. R = 90 %) for g = 15-50 deg, 0 elsewhere; realised exactly by the 6-term cosine series of eq. (34). Substrate/final medium chosen as n0 = 1.5, n6 = 1 (ratio 1.5), then all indices rescaled so the smallest is 1.\nReported: Eq. (34), p. 1261 (the design target exactly met by every Table V solution): 1/T = D^-1(g) = 4.734848 + 2.840909 cos2g - 3.314394 cos4g - 3.314394 cos6g - 0.378788 cos8g + 0.473485 cos10g, |R|^2 = 1 - 1/D^-1, where g = 2 pi n_j h_j / lambda is the phase thickness of each (equal optical thickness) layer; g = 90 deg is the quarter-wave point (here lambda = reference_nm = 1000 nm, so lambda_nm = 90000/g_deg). Values of eq. (34): R = 0.0400 at g = 0 and 90 deg (lambda -> infinity and 1000 nm; bare-substrate value for n0/n6 = 1.5); R(10 deg, 9000 nm) = 0.6732; R(15, 6000 nm) = 0.8119; R(20, 4500 nm) = 0.8735; R(30, 3000 nm) = 0.9134; R(35, 2571 nm) = 0.9132; R(40, 2250 nm) = 0.9007; R(45, 2000 nm) = 0.8696; R(50, 1800 nm) = 0.8018; R(60, 1500 nm) = 0.3788; R(70, 1286 nm) = 0.1125; R(80, 1125 nm) = 0.1666. Peak R = 0.9147 at g = 32.4 deg (2778 nm); R >= 0.9 for g 24.5-40.2 deg (3674-2239 nm); R >= 0.8 for g 14.4-50.1 deg (6263-1796 nm); secondary minimum R = 0.087 at g = 67.3 deg (1337 nm) and side lobe R = 0.184 at g = 76.8 deg (1172 nm). Fig. 4 (p. 1261) plots this curve (peak ~90 %); the paper says the reflectance curves of all solutions 'were found to be exactly in agreement with the reflectance curve of Fig. 4'. The curve depends only on g, so it repeats every 180 deg in g and is symmetric about 90 deg. Table IV row a'b'cd': Fresnel amplitude reflectances r0..r5 = 0.394025, 0.451624, -0.070191, -0.590396, -0.499886, 0.189415 with r_j = (n_(j+1) - n_j)/(n_(j+1) + n_j), r0 at the substrate interface, r5 at the incident-medium interface. Total optical thickness 5 quarter waves at reference_nm. My transfer-matrix recomputation of this stack reproduces eq. (34) to within 0.0008 in R at all g checked.\nNotes: Paper numbers media from the substrate (n0 = substrate, n1..n5 films, n6 = final medium from which light arrives, Fig. 1); reversed here so layer 1 = n5 (next to incident n6) ... layer 5 = n1 (next to substrate n0). All five layers have the same optical thickness n_j h_j; the absolute value is free, so each is set to 1 QWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm (g = 90 deg at 1000 nm, g = 90000/lambda_nm deg); scale reference_nm to move the band. Normal incidence, non-absorbing, non-dispersive indices; incident and substrate taken semi-infinite (no back surface). Printed n0/n6 = 1.49993 (target 1.5; deviation is the paper's rounding). An index printed 1.000000 inside the stack is an n = 1 layer (air-like). The reversed stack (substrate and incident swapped) gives the same R for a lossless stack; the opposite (all-primes-swapped) class in Table V is, to printed precision, the dual solution n'_j = C / n_(6-j) (reciprocal indices in reversed order, rescaled so the smallest is 1).\nLayers from Table V p. 1264 (refractive indices, row a'b'cd'); Table IV p. 1264 (surface reflectances, same row); Sec. IV pp. 1260-1263.; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 91.5 % at g = 32.4 deg; largest difference from Eq. (34) 0.0001 over g = 10-90 deg; paper R = 1 - 1/D^-1(g), Eq. (34): peak 91.47 % at g = 32.4 deg, 4 % at g = 90 deg.",
